ovipeptide you can  see that most of the t cells are not  fully activated and that the nf kappa b  resides in the cytoplasm as indicated by  the imagery  i look at a high dose of a peptide  and look at those images we get an  increase to about 32 response of 50  nanomolar ova and if i look at the  imagery you can see that the nf kappa b  is in the nucleus of the t cell  but not of the antigen presenting cell  it also has nf kappa b but it's located  in the cytoplasm so here using this this  masking and different features  we're able to isolate the t cells in  this single event and look at the the  spatial distribution of transcription  factor f kappa b  it's a very really good use of imaging  flow cytometry  so using the similarity feature i can  then calculate a ec 50 curve i can do a  dose and a time  course with that  and look at the  the temporal interactions of of you know  when and if kappa b is translocating  uh in these activated t cells  so that just is a high level overview of  some of the applications um using  imaging flow cytometry uh there's  diverse array of applications that you  can use cell signaling  internalization cell cell interaction  spot counting looking at unique uh  cell types such as parasites or ocean  water  looking at rare cells imaging  circulating tumor cells or very rare  cells that show up in your sample  so there's a lot of different types of  applications that you can use the image  stream for that helps you make novel  discoveries learn the mechanism of  action and essentially get the science  right  and with that i'd like to thank you for  your time please if you have any  questions type them in and we will  respond to you through email  thank you very much for your time during  this presentation and i look forward to  hearing from you all again in the future  thank you  you
 

